            <ArticleTitle>Epileptogenesis due to peripheral injury as a cause of focal epilepsy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1252-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report three patients in whom focal epilepsy developed shortly after painful soft tissue injuries to their hands.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case reports.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The attacks started in the injured hand. No evidence was found of an underlying brain lesion by history or from imaging studies.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">These cases suggest the possibility that the injuries led to a plastic change in the sensory/motor cortex, leading to increased excitability and ultimately to seizures. This suggests that epileptogenesis can occur in response to painful peripheral stimuli in some individuals.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
